{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Even they, even you.</b> The plain sense of the verse is: <b>even they</b> — like the Israelites who bring near; <b>even you</b> — on their behalf, just as it said earlier ״you״ etc. — ״you shall bear the iniquity״ etc." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory said in Arakhin (Arakhin 11b), and this is their language: It was taught in a baraita: ״גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם״ — you in theirs and they in yours are liable death; they in theirs are not liable death but only a warning. Abbaye said: a measurer who measured with his fellow's [utensils] is liable death — thus far [Rashi]. And Rashi wrote that Abbaye does not have this baraita that teaches ״they in theirs are not liable death,״ and he found a Tanna who agrees with him — thus far." "<b>And the Rambam</b>, of blessed memory, in chapter 3 of Hilkhot Kelei HaMikdash, wrote, and this is his language: Or if a Levite assisted [a priest] in labor that is not his labor, he is liable death, as it says ״וְלֹא יָמֻתוּ״; but a priest who performed Levitical labor is not liable death but [violates] a לֹא תַעֲשֶׂה — thus far. And it is difficult according to everything the Rambam wrote — both what he wrote, ״Or if he assisted a Levite״ etc., for according to all the explanations brought in the Gemara this is not implied. If [he ruled] according to the Tanna of the baraita, it explicitly teaches ״they in theirs are not liable death״; if according to the account of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Hananya; if according to what occurred to the Talmud to say, namely that it is a Tannaic dispute — that Rabbi Yehoshua holds a measurer who measured is [only liable by] warning and they did not decree against an assistant, while Rabbi Yochanan holds [he is liable] death and they did decree against an assistant — if so, whether according to this master or according to that master, an assistant is only a rabbinic decree, so why did the Rambam rule ״Or if he assisted a Levite etc. in labor etc. [he is] liable death״? And according to what the Talmud rejected — that all agree it is only a warning etc. — if so, one who actually measured is not liable, all the more so one who assists. And if the Rambam's position follows Abbaye, there are two answers to the matter: first, that Abbaye spoke only of one who measured, not of one who assisted, and from the language of the Talmud it is proven that there is a distinction between the one who performs [the labor] and the one who assists. Second, that Abbaye derives this liability from the verse ״וְהַזָּר הַקָּרֵב יוּמָת״ (Numbers 18:7), whereas the Rambam, of blessed memory, wrote that it is written ״וְלֹא יָמֻתוּ״ — which is the verse ״וְלֹא יָמֻתוּ גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם.״ Likewise, what the Rambam wrote regarding a priest who performed Levitical labor — that he is not liable death — is difficult, for from the baraita cited in the Talmud (Arakhin 11b) which states ״you in theirs and they in yours [are liable] death,״ and the Talmud brought no Tanna who disagrees on this matter, it is implied that this was established in their hands as fact. Why then should the Rambam disagree with the Gemara?" "<b>Now it appears</b> that the Rambam, of blessed memory, based his ruling on the baraita brought in the Sifrei (Sifrei Numbers 116), and this is their language: Rabbi Yehoshua ben Hananya once sought to assist Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgeda in closing the doors. He said to him: Step back, for you are already endangering your life — for I am of the gatekeepers and you are of the measurers — thus far. We see that a Levite is liable for the labor of another Levite, and we hear furthermore that even if he merely assists he is liable death — and this is the Rambam's own ruling." "<b>And although</b> from the Talmud the opposite is implied, the Rambam decided in accordance with the version of the Sifrei for two reasons: first, because according to the Talmud — whether according to the initial assumption or according to what the Talmud rejected, that all agree it is only a warning — whether according to Rabbi Yehoshua or according to Rabbi Yochanan, a measurer cannot serve as a gatekeeper, and they disagree only regarding an assistant. If so, what is this that Rabbi Yochanan says, ״Step back, for you are of the measurers״ etc.? Surely Rabbi Yehoshua too knows this, and he should have said merely ״Step back, for even an assistant is forbidden״ because of a rabbinic decree. And further: once we say that a measurer cannot be a gatekeeper, what difference does it make whether he gates alone or with another? Was not the labor of the gatekeepers such that they would gather as a group for the closing of doors and their opening, and all were called gatekeepers? And once we say that a measurer who measured is liable death, whether he measured alone or measured with others. [The reason] that the Talmud in Arakhin (Arakhin 11a) understood that the assistant is [forbidden only] by decree is because of the version of the baraita that it had, in which ״for you are already endangering your life״ was not mentioned, and it was difficult for the Talmud to know wherein Rabbi Yehoshua and Rabbi Yochanan disagree, and it was forced to settle their dispute in this manner — to say that by Torah law there is a prohibition only for one who performs [the labor alone] but not for an assistant, and it was the Sages who decreed concerning an assistant, and on this point Rabbi Yehoshua and Rabbi Yochanan disagree. But according to the version of the Sifrei, even an assistant is forbidden by Torah law, and the reasoning supports this reading." "<b>And even though</b> in all similar cases we follow only the version of the baraitot as cited in the Gemara, and we are not concerned with the text of the baraitot outside of it — and all the more so regarding a matter that is explicitly elucidated in the Gemara, such as what stands before us, where the Talmud says in explanation מְסַיֵּעַ גְּזֵרָה (assisting is a decree) — here there is a sound reason for this, for behold, it is before you: Abbaye who states explicitly נַקְטִינָן — a measurer who measures with his fellow's [implements] is liable to death. This would justify the version of the Sifrei, where Rabbi Yochanan said to him: you are liable for your life. For according to the version that the Talmud brought, Abbaye's opinion is rejected, as the Talmud rejected it. And even though the Talmud raised an objection to Abbaye and rejected his words, it was not before Abbaye to say אִיתּוֹתַב (it is difficult), and had Abbaye stood his ground, he would certainly have brought that [baraita] which is cited in the Sifrei." "<b>Furthermore,</b> I peeked between the eyelids of Abbaye, that upon that [passage] of the Sifrei he pitched the tent of his pavilion — from the fact that he derived the liability of death for a measurer who measures from the verse ״וְהַחוֹנִים״ etc., which is from what they derived in the baraita of the Sifrei. For there, after bringing that [teaching] of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananya etc., they said the following, and this is their language: Rabbi says: It is not necessary, for it has already been stated ״אַל תַּכְרִיתוּ״ etc. From one service to its fellow, how do we know? Scripture says ״וְהַחוֹנִים לִפְנֵי הַמִּשְׁכָּן״ etc. And what does Scripture teach by saying ״גַם הֵם גַם אַתֶּם״? Because Korach came and disputed against Aharon, Scripture warned about the entire matter — end of quote. Behold, in the baraita of the Sifrei the derashah that Abbaye stated is brought, except that in the baraita the core of the derivation — from where [is it learned] — was not spelled out, and Abbaye came and explained that it is the conclusion of the verse that is expounded, for it says ״וְהַזָּר הַקָּרֵב יוּמָת״ — in what is the verse speaking etc.? — but rather about a zar of that [particular] service — end of quote. And from this, in a similar case, we may say that the Amoraim were not expert in the baraitot, and therefore the Talmud did not know the version of the baraita as brought in the Sifrei; for had it known it, it would not have rejected the words of Abbaye, nor would it have said מְסַיֵּעַ [is merely] a decree. And you will find that the Tosafot wrote that even regarding Mishnayot we may say they were not expert — and this is according to the inclination of investigation toward the necessities at hand, and after weighing the sides of the inclination and the matter upon which we are ruling. And know that the baraitot arranged in the Mekhilta, Sifra, and Sifrei are refined, and they are not like other toseftot, and therefore the Rambam ruled according to them." "<b>And there remains</b> for us to explain according to this in what Rabbi Yehoshua and Rabbi Yochanan disagree, and it appears that Rabbi Yehoshua held that it was prohibited only from light service to severe service, such as measuring for measuring, but a measurer for a measurer — which is a service that the measurer does by himself when he grows and his voice becomes hoarse, and as the Rambam wrote there in that very chapter — Rabbi Yehoshua held that he may do so. To this Rabbi Yochanan refuted his reasoning and said to him: ״חֲזוֹר בְּךָ שֶׁאֲנִי״ etc. ״וְאַתָּה״ etc. — meaning, no person may perform the service of his fellow, but each one [does only] that for which he was appointed, and I was appointed to be a measurer and you to be a measurer, and no person does something for which he was not appointed. And therefore the Rambam, of blessed memory, was wise and ruled the particulars of this law there and said, and this is his language: that the measurer shall not assist the measurer, nor the measurer the measurer — end of quote — to exclude the reasoning of Rabbi Yehoshua, who held [it was permitted] when one came to assist, [against which] Rabbi Yochanan [argued]." "<b>Again</b> I returned to examine the words of the Rambam, so as not to be contrary to the reasoning of the Gemara regarding the law of מְסַיֵּעַ (one who assists). For there are two types of מְסַיֵּעַ: one is מְסַיֵּעַ in a service that has its full complement [of workers] for that service aside from him, such as if there were measurers sufficient for that gate and he came to assist — this one is liable only rabbinically. And the second is מְסַיֵּעַ to complete the number needed for that service — and this one is liable by Torah law. And therefore the Talmud, on account of accommodating the reasoning of Rabbi Yehoshua according to the version that was before it, held that there were measurers sufficient for that service, and therefore Rabbi Yehoshua held regarding assisting that there is no prohibition in the matter. But according to the version of the Sifrei, which says to him ״אַתָּה מִתְחַיֵּב בְּנַפְשְׁךָ״ (you are liable for your life), we are compelled to explain that there were not enough measurers to push the doors. And therefore the Rambam was precise in his words, and this is his language: the Levites etc. are warned that no one shall do the melachah of his fellow, that the measurer shall not assist etc. — end of quote. Behold, from the fact that he was careful to say ״מְלֶאכֶת חֲבֵרוֹ״ (the melachah of his fellow) and did not say ״בִּמְלֶאכֶת חֲבֵרוֹ״ (in the melachah of his fellow), he indicates that this מְסַיֵּעַ enters into a portion of the service specially designated for his fellow, and it says ״שֶׁלֹּא יְסַיֵּעַ״ — meaning, and this is the framework: that I prohibit to you the assistance in it, and therefore he did not say ״וְשֶׁלֹּא יְסַיֵּעַ,״ and understand." "<b>We thus find ourselves saying</b> that the מְסַיֵּעַ in a service that does not lack its own [sufficient] workers is liable to death — according to the opinion of the Rambam and the baraita of the Sifrei — and there is no contradiction from the Talmud to this. Except that it remains for us to know the reason of the Rambam, of blessed memory: why did he not derive the liability of death for a Levite measurer who measures from the verse ״וְהַחוֹנִים״ etc. ״וְהַזָּר הַקָּרֵב יוּמָת,״ as stated in the baraita of the Sifrei and as per the words of Abbaye, as I wrote above? Instead, he went to seek out a different verse, and from his words it appears that it is the verse ״אַל תַּכְרִיתוּ מִשְׁפְּחוֹת הַקְּהָתִי״ at the end of parashat BaMidbar. For there two statements were made, which the Rambam brought in specifying this law: the first, ״אִישׁ אִישׁ עַל עֲבוֹדָתוֹ״ etc., and the second, ״וְלֹא יָמוּתוּ.״ And even though he wrote ״וְעַל מַשָּׂאוֹ,״ whereas in the verse ״אַל תַּכְרִיתוּ״ it says ״וְאֶל מַשָּׂאוֹ,״ this is certainly a scribal error, for why would he leave what is stated in the verse where it says ״וְלֹא יָמוּתוּ״ and go searching for a verse in parashat Naso, where it says ״וְעַל מַשָּׂאוֹ״ but where it does not say ״וְיָמוּתוּ״? Rather, one must certainly emend in the Rambam [to read] ״וְאֶל מַשָּׂאוֹ.״ Be that as it may, it is difficult: why did he turn away from the words of the Tannaim and Amoraim?" "<b>And it will be seen</b> that the Rambam took the derashah of the Tanna who expounded from the verse ״גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם״ along these lines: ״גַּם הֵם״ — Levites [who perform] the service of the priests; ״גַּם אַתֶּם״ — priests [who perform] the service of the Levites. And from the inclusio of the word ״גַּם,״ which need not have said anything other than ״הֵם וְאַתֶּם,״ from this he derives the obligation [of death] upon a Levite who performs a service that is not his. And what the Rambam wrote — ״or if a Levite assisted, etc., they are liable to death, as it is said: וְלֹא יָמֻתוּ״ — this refers to what is stated in the verse: ״וְלֹא יָמֻתוּ גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם.״ And this derashah Rabbi [Yehudah HaNasi] also concedes, for although he said ״it is not necessary,״ he nevertheless concluded: ״What does the verse come to teach? גַּם הֵם and so forth — because [Korach] objected, etc., the verse went back and warned,״ etc. — thus he justified the derashah of the first Tanna. And although Abayye expounded from the verse ״וְהַזָּר הַקָּרֵב,״ which is Rabbi's derashah, since it has been revealed to us that Rabbi does not contradict the derashah of ״וְלֹא יָמֻתוּ״ as noted, the Rambam chose the proof from ״וְלֹא יָמֻתוּ,״ which is more explicit than the derashah of ״וְהַזָּר״ and so forth — whose derashah proceeds by way of ״if this is not the topic,״ for the verse is said about Moses and Aaron, as it is written ״וְהַחֹנִים קֵדְמָה מִזְרָחָה מֹשֶׁה״ and so forth. And the discerning one will understand that it is not an explicit derashah; therefore the Rambam chose to bring in his book the verse ״וְלֹא יָמֻתוּ גַּם הֵם״ and so forth." "<b>And as for</b> what he brought a proof for the warning against [a priest performing] service to [Levitical] service from the verse ״אִישׁ אִישׁ עַל עֲבֹדָתוֹ וְעַל מַשָּׂאוֹ״ — it is because without this, whence would we have a warning against [crossing] from service to service upon which the punishment would fall? And whence would the force of the prohibition in this matter arise to explain the statement ״וְלֹא יָמוּתוּ״ in the case of a Levite who slaughters? Therefore he brought the verse ״אִישׁ אִישׁ עַל עֲבֹדָתוֹ וְעַל מַשָּׂאוֹ.״ And it is possible that our master (the Rambam) is explaining the words of Rabbi that I wrote above, and this is his language: ״It is not necessary, for it has already been said ״אַל תַּכְרִיתוּ״ and so forth, which concerns the matter of the offering of R. Yehoshua to assist in the closing of the gates — for there it says ״אַל תַּכְרִיתוּ״ and so forth, and there it says ״אִישׁ אִישׁ עַל עֲבֹדָתוֹ״ and so forth. And what he said at the end, ״from service to service, whence?״ — this does not refer back to the warning but to the liability: whence [do we know] that one is liable to death? And according to this, the words of the Rambam are the words of Rabbi, and he, of blessed memory, brings in his book the more clearly elucidated derashah." "<b>And according to this approach</b> as well, one must emend in the words of the Rambam: ״וְאֶל מַשָּׂאוֹ.״ And as for what the Rambam ruled, that a priest who performed the service of a Levite is not liable to death — it appears that he judged according to what has preceded us, that the verse needs to state both the punishment and the warning, and here in this verse no warning and no [liability of] death were stated except for the Levites, as it is written: ״לֹא יִקְרְבוּ וְלֹא יָמוּתוּ.״ And although he concluded: ״גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם,״ and added the priests in what is said, there is no necessity that the verse reverts to both for a warning and a liability of death — and we need not say anything other than that it comes for the warning, since no warning was stated for the priests regarding the service of the Levites except here. And this is not an instance of the principle ״there is no hekesh for halves,״ and it can be explained along these lines: ״גַּם הֵם״ — concerning what was mentioned nearby; ״גַּם אַתֶּם״ — you [too] have a warning regarding this matter. Further, from the fact that Rabbi said ״it is not necessary״ and [that] ״גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם״ was said only because Korach objected — from this you learn that there is no death penalty for a priest who performs the service of a Levite, for if that were not so, then it would be both necessary and necessary, for the verses that Rabbi cited do not contain in them a liability of death for a priest in Levitical service but only a warning, as is clear there from the baraita. Rather, it is certain that the statement ״גַּם אַתֶּם״ comes only for a warning, and therefore the Tanna of the Sifri likewise said only ״priests regarding the service of Levites, whence?״ — and did not say ״priests who are punished and warned regarding the service of Levites, whence?״ as he began by saying regarding the Levites." "<b>And as for</b> what they said in the baraita brought in Arakhin: ״You [priests] in theirs [the Levites' service] and they [Levites] in yours [the priests' service] are in [liability of] death״ — and the Gemara did not bring the opposing baraita, this indicates that this baraita was accepted by the masters of the Gemara. I say that when we stand on the depth of the sugya, from it we will be compelled to follow the path of the Rambam. And it is that there it concludes in that same baraita, and this is their language: ״They in theirs are not in [liability of] death but in a warning,״ and Rashi explained: ״They in theirs — a Levite who closes doors,״ end of quote. And it is difficult for me regarding what the Gemara asks shortly after, according to the words of Abayye who said that a Levite who slaughters is in [liability of] death — and this is their language: ״They objected: a Levite who slaughters and a gatekeeper who sang are not in [liability of] death but in a warning,״ etc., end of quote. It is difficult: why does the Talmud seek out another baraita to challenge the words of Abayye, and did not challenge from the very baraita immediately preceding it, which said ״they in theirs are not in [liability of] death״? And further, it is difficult: this particular detail of a Levite [crossing] from service to service — where was it mentioned that one should say about him that he is not in [liability of] death but in a warning? Did the Tanna not come only to explain the statement ״גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם״? And in any case, if the Tanna intends to expound from this verse also [the case of] a Levite [crossing] from service to service — for this too is included in the statement ״גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם״ — if so, the ruling that applies to a priest who performed Levitical service should apply as well to a Levite who performed another Levite's service, since the Creator included both of them together in this statement." "<b>And if</b> you say that one cannot derive from this verse anything beyond a warning, then likewise one cannot hold the priest liable [to death] for the service of a Levite — so you are forced to say that the Tanna does not expound the prohibition for a Levite regarding his fellow's service from the verse ״גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם.״ If so, where does the Tanna stand when he says ״they in theirs״? Rather, it is certain that this compels us to say that the word ״הֵם״ is an error, and we should read in its place ״אַתֶּם,״ and this is its meaning: ״They [Levites] in yours [the priests' service] are in [liability of] death, and you [priests] in theirs are not in [liability of] death but in a warning.״ Also missing from the baraita is ״they in yours,״ for since the printer saw two duplicated words, ״they in yours״ and ״they in yours,״ he thought it was a duplication and therefore omitted one of them. And this is the correct text of the baraita: ״גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם״ — you in theirs and they in yours; they in yours are in [liability of] death, you in theirs are not in [liability of] death but in a warning. And therefore the Talmud did not find [it fitting] to challenge Abayye from this baraita, which contains no mention of two [different] Levitical services, and challenged from another baraita. Also by this reading, the latter clause of the baraita will be in the derashah of the verse ״גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם״ that the Tanna brought, and he said that Levites in [the service of] priests are in [liability of] death, but priests in [the service of] Levites are only in a warning — and the reason is as I wrote in explanation of the Rambam's words. And from this point onward, the ruling of the Rambam and the Sifri and the baraita of Arakhin rise together in a single reed."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {7 "The two Levitical roles being discussed are שׁוֹעֵר (a measurer of Levitical dues) and מְשׁוֹרֵר (a singer); the distinction between them and the nature of each service follows the halakhic context in b. Yevamot and Rambam, Hilkhot Klei HaMikdash.", 14 "גָרְסִינַן, literally 'we read,' refers to the correct text of the Gemara as opposed to the printed edition; the final idiom בְּקָנֶה אֶחָד ('in one reed') means they align perfectly, like reeds growing in a single straight row."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:27.413996677Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}